Arcline Combat

A server-authoritative combat and ability system for Roblox, written in strict Luau as a single self-contained server script.

The whole file is built around one rule: a client may ask for an ability and provide an aim direction — nothing else. It never supplies an origin, target, hit list, damage value or cooldown. Those values are resolved by the server from authoritative state.

Hit detection, damage, cooldowns, rate limiting, movement restrictions and knockback are all resolved server-side.

Scope: this is a public demonstration project created to show architecture, Roblox API usage and defensive server-side programming in a readable amount of code. It shares design principles with a private framework maintained for a published game, but contains none of its source, IDs or configuration.

What it demonstrates
Area	Where to look
Service lifecycle (Init / Start / Shutdown)	Framework
Custom signal with deferred compaction	Signal
Ownership-scoped cleanup	Bin, PlayerStateService
Remote validation & untrusted input parsing	CombatService:Execute, sanitizeDirection
Token-bucket rate limiting	PlayerStateService:ConsumeToken
Per-ability cooldowns	CombatService:Execute
Declarative state machine	STATE_RULES, PlayerStateService:SetState
Cone melee spatial query	CombatService:_resolveArc
Raycast + distance-based damage falloff	CombatService:_resolveRay
CFrame object-space math	CombatService:_resolveArc
Server-controlled knockback and dash	CombatService:_applyHit, _resolveMobility
Player / character lifecycle	PlayerStateService
Static configuration validation	REQUIRED_FIELDS
Runtime demo generation	DemoService
Server-side telemetry	TelemetryService
Abilities

Abilities are data rather than separate execution paths. Each entry specifies a Kind, and each kind maps to exactly one resolver.

Ability	Kind	Behaviour
Cleave	Arc	65° cone melee. Uses a bounded overlap query followed by an object-space cone test and per-character deduplication.
Bolt	Ray	95-stud hitscan with linear damage falloff based on the actual impact point.
Lunge	Mobility	Grounded-only forward dash using a clamped server-side velocity result.
Architecture
CONFIG (frozen)
├── Signal
├── Bin
└── Framework
    ├── PlayerStateService
    │   ├── Player lifecycle
    │   ├── Character lifecycle
    │   ├── State machine
    │   └── Token bucket
    │
    ├── CombatService
    │   ├── Remote endpoints
    │   ├── Input validation
    │   ├── Cooldowns
    │   ├── Ability resolvers
    │   └── Demo tools
    │
    ├── TelemetryService
    │   ├── Damage tracking
    │   ├── Rejection logging
    │   └── Execution logging
    │
    └── DemoService
        ├── Training arena
        ├── Arena walls
        ├── Training targets
        └── Debug visuals

Both input front-ends — the RemoteEvent used by a real client and the server-created demo Tools — funnel into the same CombatService:Execute entry point.

Validation belongs to the service rather than the transport layer, so adding another input method does not create a second security implementation.

Validation pipeline

Validation runs cheapest-first and stops at the first failure:

type check
    ↓
ability lookup
    ↓
player state
    ↓
loadout ownership
    ↓
token bucket
    ↓
aim sanitization
    ↓
character / humanoid liveness
    ↓
action state
    ↓
ability cooldown
    ↓
movement restrictions
    ↓
commit cooldown + state
    ↓
server-side resolver

This means a request flood is rejected before an expensive spatial or physics query is performed.

Installation
Open Roblox Studio and create a Baseplate place.
Create one Script inside ServerScriptService.
Paste the contents of ArclineCombat.lua into that Script.
Press Play.

Nothing else is required.

The script provisions its own:

ReplicatedStorage/ArclineNet
AbilityRequest (RemoteEvent)
CombatFeedback (RemoteEvent)
QueryLoadout (RemoteFunction)
Cleave, Bolt and Lunge demo Tools
training arena
six server-created training targets
Testing
Normal gameplay

Press Play and equip one of the generated tools.

Cleave

Damages targets inside the server-calculated cone.
Each character is damaged at most once per swing.
Applies clamped knockback.

Bolt

Performs a server-side raycast.
Uses the actual impact position to calculate damage falloff.
Stops at the first blocking hit.

Lunge

Applies a forward impulse.
Can only be triggered while grounded.
The resulting velocity is clamped server-side.

The player's running damage total and rejected-request count are visible through leaderstats.

Server-authority testing

The remote endpoint intentionally accepts untrusted values so its validation can be tested directly.

For example:

local net = game:GetService("ReplicatedStorage").ArclineNet

-- Valid request
net.AbilityRequest:FireServer(
    "Cleave",
    Vector3.new(0, 0, -1)
)

-- Invalid ability
net.AbilityRequest:FireServer(
    "Nuke",
    Vector3.zAxis
)

-- Invalid argument type
net.AbilityRequest:FireServer(
    123,
    nil
)

-- Invalid vertical aim
net.AbilityRequest:FireServer(
    "Bolt",
    Vector3.new(0, -1, 0)
)

-- Trigger the request-rate limiter
for _ = 1, 40 do
    net.AbilityRequest:FireServer(
        "Bolt",
        Vector3.zAxis
    )
end

-- Read server-owned loadout / cooldown information
print(
    net.QueryLoadout:InvokeServer()
)

Every rejected request is logged by the server with its reason and a running rejection count for that player.

The client only receives the result of its own request through CombatFeedback.

Technical notes
Strict Luau

The script uses --!strict and explicit types for its major state records, ability definitions and service boundaries.

Timing

os.clock() is used for cooldown and state timing because it provides a monotonic process clock. Combat expiration therefore does not depend on wall-clock changes.

Server-side physics

Knockback and mobility values originate from server-owned configuration and are clamped before being applied.

Cleanup

Per-player resources are registered in a Bin when created. Character-scoped resources are destroyed when the character changes, while player-scoped resources are destroyed when the player leaves.

Configuration validation

Ability configuration is validated once during startup. Each configured ability must reference a supported resolver and contain the fields required by its Kind.

Public demonstration

This repository is intentionally limited to a single public demonstration script.

It is not a copy of the author's private framework. The architecture and implementation patterns are presented here only to demonstrate Luau engineering, Roblox API knowledge and server-authoritative game-system design.
